    ["Does an index on (A, B) help a query with 'GROUP BY B' without filtering on A?", "Generally no, because B-Tree indexes are sorted by A first; B alone cannot be read sequentially without skip-scans", ["Yes, the index order does not matter", "Yes, any column in the index works equally", "Only if descending"], "Leftmost prefix rule requires the leading column A to be present for standard index scans."],
    ["What does 'GROUP BY GROUPING SETS ((department), (team), ())' produce?", "Subtotals by department, subtotals by team, and a grand total row", ["Cross product of department and team", "Only the grand total", "Error"], "It computes three specific aggregation levels in a single query."],
    ["Can you group by JSON fields in PostgreSQL?", "Yes, by extracting a scalar value, e.g. GROUP BY data->>'category'", ["No, JSON cannot be referenced in SQL", "Only by converting to XML", "Only in MongoDB"], "Extracting text or integers from JSON using operators like ->> allows standard grouping."],
    ["Can you group by JSON columns in MySQL?", "You can group by JSON expressions or generated columns: GROUP BY JSON_UNQUOTE(JSON_EXTRACT(doc, '$.type'))", ["No, MySQL forbids all JSON functions in GROUP BY", "Only in MySQL 5.5", "Only with memory tables"], "MySQL allows grouping on expressions that extract scalar values from JSON documents."],
    ["What is the maximum number of grouping columns allowed in SQL Server?", "32 grouping columns in a GROUP BY clause", ["8 columns", "256 columns", "Unlimited"], "Microsoft SQL Server limits the number of expressions in a GROUP BY clause to 32."]
